@import "./compiler.sly"
@import "./build.sly"
@use Check

class SlytherInstanceManifest {
    What a check remembers about every instance, written as manifest.json in .slyther/instances and keyed
    by `kind:name`, contains for each one:

    - specHash: The hash of the #{Compiler::SlytherArtifact} that declares the instance and of every
      artifact it references that has no code of its own: a kind, whose hash is its rules, a plain
      artifact, or an instance of a composite kind. So a change to its prose or its args is noticed, and
      so is a change to what it leans on
    - rulesHash: The scope hash of its kind, which is its rules and what they reference, so a change to
      what every instance of the kind must do is noticed by each of them. Absent in a manifest written
      before version 2
    - parent: The instance whose expand emitted it, when it was not declared by hand
    - locatedWith: The args locate was run with, when it takes more than the id, so the instance can be
      found again once its declaration is gone or its args changed
    - contentHash: The hash of the segments locate prints, each as its path and its content, sorted, so
      where a segment is never matters, only what it holds. The content of a folder is its listing, one
      entry per line with a slash after the folders, so it changes with its structure and not with its files
    - signature: The `key shape` lines signature printed, when the kind prints them
    - dependencies: For every instance it references, the content hash and the signature it had when
      this one was evaluated
    - evaluatedWith: The hash of every script and prompt that takes part in evaluating an instance of the
      kind, as built: locate, signature, uses and evaluate
    - result: pass, fail, missing or orphan, and errors: what evaluate reported

    It records the version it was written with, 2, so one written before is brought forward: every
    instance is taken to have been brought in line with the rules of its kind as they are now, so what it
    already complies with is not updated again and only a change made from then on is noticed. Loading a
    manifest that does not exist gives an empty one at the current version, and it is saved with its
    version and with its instances sorted.
}

class SlytherInstanceChecker {
    Checks every instance a #{Compiler::ParsedSlytherScript} declares against the rules of its kind, given
    the root of the project, the folder the operations are built into, the path of the
    #{SlytherInstanceManifest}, the #{Build::SlytherArtifactManifest} and a #{Build::SlytherGenerator}.

    An instance is every artifact whose kind has operations, identified by its name, and the instances it
    references with \#{} are its dependencies. Instances are checked in an order where every one comes
    after its dependencies, so a dependency is evaluated, and fixed, before what depends on it.

    An instance of a composite kind is expanded before any instance is ordered: its expand operation runs
    with its params filled as create fills them, what it prints is read with #{Compiler::SlytherExpansion}
    and written as `expanded/kind/name.sly` next to the manifest, so what it emits is committed and
    reviewed, and the emitted instances are checked as any other, in the order their references give.
    When what it prints is not valid, the instance fails with the reason and emits nothing. Since it has
    no code to evaluate, it is reported as expanded, with how many instances it emitted, and every
    emitted instance is recorded with it as its parent and reported under it.

    An instance the manifest knows by a parent that no longer emits it, or whose parent is gone, is an
    orphan: it leaves the graph, so it is neither created, updated nor evaluated, its code is not
    touched, an instance that references it fails as it does when a reference is missing, and it is
    reported as orphan on every check until its code is gone, when it is forgotten. Nothing removes an
    orphan yet.

    Whatever the generator is asked about an instance, to create, update or evaluate it, is followed by
    what the instance references, one level deep, so its prose may lean on it: the rules of a kind, the
    prose of a plain artifact, and the args, the prose and the signature, when its kind prints one, of an
    instance. What the referenced ones reference is not included: an instance that needs it references
    it itself. The prose of a `ref` is only a pointer to its file, which the generator reads with its tools.

    It runs in one of two modes: with compile it is a compiler, and brings every instance in line with
    its declaration; without it, nothing touches the code, so it only evaluates and reports.

    For every instance, locate says where it is, run with the id and with whatever else its params ask of
    the instance, filled as create fills them, and so are signature and uses: when it exits 1 the instance
    is missing, and, with
    compile, when its kind has a create operation, it is created and then evaluated, otherwise nothing
    else is done. When it exits 0 but prints a segment that is not there, or a range of lines of a
    folder, the instance fails with what it printed and what locate must print instead, and nothing is
    created over it, since where it is was never established; the rest of the instances go on, so one
    kind whose locate is wrong never stops a build.

    Otherwise its content hash, its signature and what it uses are computed, and it is
    checked for change: it changed when it was never evaluated, when its spec hash changed, when the
    rules of its kind changed, when its content hash changed, when what evaluates it changed, when it
    failed last time and compile is on, or when a dependency changed in a way it uses.

    Whatever changed is brought in line with the update operation of its kind before it is evaluated,
    when compile is on and its kind has one, since the prose rules over the code and the evaluate of a
    kind only ever covers part of what the prose says: an evaluate that passes says the code met what
    that evaluate checks, never that it still says what the prose says. Two are not brought in line: an
    instance with no record yet, which has changed from nothing and is evaluated as it stands, and one
    that uses a key that is gone, which no update can fix. Without compile, a spec hash or a rules hash
    that changed is not recorded as seen, so a later build still updates the instance.

    For a dependency, the signature it had when
    this instance was evaluated is compared with the one it has now, key by key, into the keys whose
    shape changed or that are gone, and the keys that are new. What the instance uses of the dependency
    decides: a key, when that key changed; `*`, when any key changed; `**`, when any key changed or was
    added. Without uses, `*` is assumed, and without signatures, any change of the content hash of the
    dependency counts. A key the instance uses that is gone fails it without evaluating, since it is
    certainly broken. Whatever evaluates it and passes is recorded, so the next check keeps it, and an
    instance that failed is reported as failing until it changes.

    When the args of an instance move it, so locate was run with other args when it was last checked, it
    is located once more with those args, and whatever they still find is reported as an orphan and left
    alone, since nothing removes code. The instance itself goes on as any other, at where it is located
    now, and the move is reported once, by the check that notices it.

    Evaluating runs the evaluate operation of the kind: every deterministic step as its script with the
    id, failing with what it prints when it exits with a code other than 0, then every llm step as its
    markdown followed by what the instance must do, its args and its prose as declared, and the segments
    of its code, asked from the generator for a verdict of pass and errors. With compile, an instance
    that fails is updated with the update operation of its kind and evaluated once more.

    Create and update run with their params filled from the instance, by the name of each param: id is
    the name of the instance, errors is the errors it is run to fix, a param named as an arg of the
    instance is the value of that arg, and any other is the prose of the instance; a param that is not
    optional and gets nothing must throw an error. A deterministic one runs its scripts in order, handed
    the params as their args, and throws when one fails.

    Any other is its markdown, executed by the generator, and is handed the params twice: substituted
    where the markdown wrote them in braces, and as the instance it is run on, named with its kind and
    followed by the value of every param and by what the instance must do, as declared. The substitution
    alone is not enough, since nothing makes a markdown mention a param, and one that never does would
    otherwise run without ever being told which instance it is working on. The errors are appended in a
    section of their own when there are any, so they are not repeated among the params. After either,
    the instance is located again.

    What passes is reported as created, updated, fixed or pass, by what was done to it; what fails as
    fail; what is up to date as kept; and what is missing and cannot be created as missing. With max,
    checking refuses to create, update or evaluate more instances with the llm than allowed, before
    doing any.

    Every id list prints that the script does not declare is reported as a warning.
}
